
<p><strong>Projectile motion is the motion of an object moving in a plane, with an initial velocity V<sub>0</sub> at an angle to the x-axis.</strong></p>

<p>Projectile motion is a complex motion that can be divided into two independent components: vertical and horizontal. 
While the horizontal component of the motion remains constant throughout, the vertical component decreases by g·t due 
to the gravitational force pulling the ball downward (free fall). On the Earth's surface, gravitational acceleration is approximately g = 9.81 m/s².</p>

<p>The following formulas describe projectile motion:</p>

<p>Velocity in the x-direction: <strong>v<sub>0X</sub> = v<sub>0</sub> · cos(α)</strong></p>

<p>Velocity in the y-direction: <strong>v<sub>0Y</sub> = v<sub>0</sub> · sin(α) - g·t</strong></p>

<p>Distance traveled in the x-direction: <strong>x = v<sub>0</sub> · t · cos(α)</strong></p>

<p>Distance traveled in the y-direction: <strong>y = v<sub>0</sub> · t · sin(α) - (g·t²)/2</strong></p>

<p>Time to reach the highest point of the trajectory: <strong>t = v<sub>0</sub> · sin(α) / g</strong></p>

<p>Maximum height reached: <strong>H<sub>Max</sub> = (v<sub>0</sub>² · sin²(α)) / (2·g)</strong></p>

<p>Range: <strong>D = (v<sub>0</sub>² · sin(2·α)) / g</strong></p>
